6. BOUNDARIES
==================================================================================================

REPRESENTATION  079 leaves the structural form to 080. It "decides none of 080's choices: no
                schema grammar, element names, role-list representation, owning-role syntax,
                direction tokens, cardinality notation, defaults, reference-carrier structure,
                version fields" (079 §21), and "How roles are named or identified is 080's." (079
                §7). The choices, each an 080 CHOICE and not a Blueprint or RMS fact:
                (1) roles as an unordered list of entries, each with a local key, a stated
                    meaning and its own eligibility — so that roles are declared, distinguishable
                    and each has a meaning;
                (2) role.key compared exactly, unique ignoring case, never a global code — so that
                    a designation is never read two ways;
                (3) owning_role as one designation by role.key — one carrier, so that case 3 of
                    §4 is NATIVE and no two carriers can disagree;
                (4) direction as an optional ordered pair of role keys, from and to, required
                    only on a two-role World type (§4 S-04) — so that direction is stated apart
                    from the owning role without a direction vocabulary; eligibility likewise
                    required, at least one entry per role, only on a two-role World type. Both
                    requirements represent §13.3's "carrying … its direction … and its
                    legality constraints"; neither carrier is named by a source;
                (5) eligibility as a declaration reference — the declared Model and the declared
                    Kind themselves, the form 079 RF-079-1 describes and 069, 073 and 077 use — not
                    an R → R reference to a KIND-DEFINITION or MODEL-DEFINITION Record, and not
                    both. The declaration form needs no definition Record to exist first and no
                    Registry Record identifier while no Registry Kind code is bound (row 117). An
                    R → R carrier is not unconstitutional; it is not chosen, and if a governed
                    revision adopts one it is classified as R → R (Artifact 063 §8.1), with its
                    dependency status left to 062 and row 111 (079 §21 item 6);
                (6) no model-scope element — the governing model is EXTERNAL evidence (§4 S-01);
                (7) no name element — a name is definition content, not identity;
                (8) no relationship-type cardinality carrier — the omission route of 079 §9.3.
                None is offered as the only representation a governing source could establish. A
                later governed revision may change one; this contract does not pre-empt it.

DECLARATION     079 §8.5 keeps four statements apart, and this contract represents only the first:
AND             A — definition validity: participant roles and exactly one owning role are
APPLICATION         declared — role and owning_role, with §4 O-01 to O-06;
                B — the model's relationship architecture decides which connections are typed by
                    this family — not represented; §4 S-01 consults it as evidence;
                C — where Relationship Records are used, the endpoint occupying the owning role
                    holds the edge (I-73, "Wherever a Record Model uses Relationship Records";
                    §13.9) — instance behaviour, rows 197–199 and 224;
                D — elsewhere the application is open (OD-079-2) — not represented, and not a
                    ground for any outcome (§4 S-05).
                A definition used by a model without Relationship Records carries the same
                owning_role; nothing here makes that model use one (079 RT-079-11, RT-079-25).

CARDINALITY     Not represented in this revision (§3 NOT REPRESENTED). OD-079-1 — what a type's
                cardinality bounds — belongs to the Blueprint/RMS author, with no Roadmap row
                assigned (079 §19.1; 079 SC-079-T). If an authoritative source closes it, this
                contract changes through its own governed revision and cites the closure; it does
                not close it here. Until then: no type set may present a cardinality as
                checkable under this contract; rows 112 and 116 have no cardinality to validate
                from it; no model mechanism may cite it to enforce a bound on edges (079 §9.3
                "What OD-079-1 blocks").

RESOLUTION      A structurally valid Record carries the baseline Record identity that later
                Registry-definition resolution requires (§2): partition, kind and object_id under
                [PARTITION]-[KIND]-[OBJECT_ID]-[SLUG]. Artifact 080 does not establish the
                complete id+version resolution representation: rows 108–110 retain authority over
                version representation, row 114 owns "create/resolve/version a definition", row
                115 owns "consumer resolves by id+version", and row 117 binds Registry codes.
                Resolution never relies on slug, a type name or a role key. A role key resolving
                within one Record is local referential integrity (§4), not Registry resolution.
                This contract implements no resolver, lookup, index, registration or minting, and
                defines no lookup of a type by name. Resolvable is not legal (Artifact 063 §11).
                (079 RS-079-1 to RS-079-5)

VERSIONABILITY  Nothing here prevents the Registry evolution and versioning model of rows 108–110
                from applying to a RELATIONSHIP-TYPE-DEFINITION. Artifact 080 defines no version
                identifier, token syntax, revision numbering, pinning, supersession link,
                deprecation state, compatibility class, migration, retention or temporal
                lifecycle; those stay with rows 108 (evolution: "temporal account without a World
                History Record"), 109 (versioning, pinning) and 110 (supersession, deprecation).
                Three things are kept apart: an element this revision does not support (P-01A,
                P-01B — not conforming today); an element a later governed revision may introduce
                once rows 108–110 establish it (not prohibited); an eighth universal envelope
                field (prohibited, P-05). A definition's version history is not edge history:
                revising a type re-owns, rewrites or revalidates no edge by itself, and any
                consequence for existing edges is left to rows 108–110 and the owning model (079
                VS-079-3). Versionable therefore means: Artifact 080 does not structurally
                obstruct the architecture rows 108–110 own. (079 VS-079-1 to VS-079-3)

REFERENCES      One concrete reference relation: eligibility, a declaration reference to a
                declared Record Model and a declared Kind of it (079 RF-079-1). Kept apart: a
                declared Kind (named in role.eligibility.kind); a KIND-DEFINITION Record (not
                named); a domain Record of that Kind (never named); a declared Model (named in
                role.eligibility.model); a MODEL-DEFINITION Record (not named). No W, E, P, V or I
                domain instance, no domain state and no runtime instance is a target, a
                dependency or an authority, in a structured part or in definition content (079
                RF-079-2 to RF-079-4; RMS §10.3: "R → R definitions = ALLOWED. R → domain
                instances = FORBIDDEN."). A reference transfers no ownership, admission,
                mutation authority, canonicality or semantic authority, is not by itself a
                semantic dependency, and is not legal because it resolves (RF-079-8, RF-079-9).
                An undetermined target is not legal: "Undetermined is not permitted." (Artifact
                063 RB-6). Admissible category is not a family relation: no other reference is
                carried (P-02). No inbound type reference is defined (P-11).

LAYER           Artifact 062 places "relationship type and participant-role semantics" in L1
                stratum 1b, which may depend on RFS and must not depend on L2–L5 (062 §6, D-8).
                Naming a declared Kind does not, by itself, establish a semantic dependency (D-9).
                Where a concrete relation is also a semantic dependency, 062 and row 111 govern
                it; this contract carries no dependency element and designs no graph (P-09).

EXECUTION       "Registry does not execute schemas." (RMS §10.2). No element of this contract
                creates, moves, mutates, projects or validates a relationship, holds validator
                state or runs a check (079 RT-079-22). The NATIVE checks of this file are contract
                statements, not an implemented validator. The Blueprint names JSON Schema as the
                adopted structural-validation realization and LinkML as an authoring aid (§12.15,
                §26.3b); this file is neither and generates neither (SC-080-K).

SOVEREIGNTY     The Registry governs the definition; each Record Model owns its relationships and
                decides whether it uses Relationship Records at all (Blueprint §13.6e; RMS §15:
                "Model relationships are not identical and are never made so."). A definition
                naming World Kinds gives the Registry no World Record or edge. A relationship type
                is neither a field nor a reference, and this family makes no field or reference a
                relationship (079 RT-079-17). The family's existence brings no reference,
                dependency, workflow transition or composition link under it: which connections a
                model types through this family is that model's architecture (079 §8.5 B; PI-38
                of 079).

LS              Row 080 carries LS: LS-1 and Why: LS-3. Roadmap PART III defines LS-1 as "every
                Kind spec ↔ its Registry KIND-DEFINITION" and LS-3 as "Relationship ↔
                Relationship-Type-Definition — including the owning-role declaration". This file is
                neither a Kind specification nor a KIND-DEFINITION, and it is not a relationship.
                Both values are preserved; no LS-1 pair, no 079 ↔ 080 pair and no new lockstep
                class is invented. The LS-3 pairs are formed downstream, where a model's
                relationships meet their type definitions (rows 197–198, 276–277, 338, 356, 366)
                (SC-080-E, SC-080-F).

MATERIALIZATION This file is an authored structural contract. A RELATIONSHIP-TYPE-DEFINITION Record
                conforming to it is committed to canon/registry/** only through the Mutation
                Coordinator after G-CANON-R (Roadmap §0.8, PART X); the type sets that author such
                Records — rows 198, 277, 338, 356 and 366 — carry G: G-CANON-R. Nothing here
                authors or commits any Record. AD-REG-SOURCE-ENVELOPE does not apply to this file
                or relax it: that ruling concerns the canonicalization sources Roadmap §0.8 names —
                rows 075, 085, 105, 106 and 107 — and this file is neither such a source nor a
                Record. Every element of §1 stays REQUIRED as §2–§3 state; no envelope field
                becomes optional on a candidate or a materialized Record.

DOWNSTREAM      Unlocks are not dependencies. Row 080's → names 177, 277, 338, 352, 375, preserved
                as written. The rows that list 080 in H are 198, 277, 338, 356 and 366. Neither
                list is rewritten to match the other (SC-080-J).

                Unlock  Row's own responsibility and Val                 Treatment by 080
